WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:05.430 line:100%
The world

2
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.980 line:100%
will remember this day.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.230 line:100%
The Trinity Test and 
the revelation

4
00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.780 line:100%
of the first atomic
explosion to the world

5
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.660 line:100%
was one of the most important
moments in 20th century.

6
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.200 line:100%
The Trinity Test and the
development and the race

7
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.290 line:100%
and the fact
that it was all

8
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.080 line:100%
attempting to save kind 
of humanity from fascism.

9
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.250 line:100%
What was really interesting
was going back

10
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:31.090 line:100%
and trying to understand

11
00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:32.380 line:100%
what these guys were thinking

12
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.920 line:100%
and the terrible decisions that
were placed in front of them.

13
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.220 line:100%
I knew this was going to be

14
00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:40.510 line:100%
one of the most important things
to technically figure out.

15
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.300 line:100%
When Chris makes a film,

16
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.810 line:100%
he wants to do
as much as possible,

17
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.810 line:100%
practically, and it's absolutely

18
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:50.020 line:100%
all about creating a world that
the audience can identify with.

19
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.020 line:100%
In the start, everybody is
just scared shitless.

20
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.110 line:100%
Everybody resets sequence.

21
00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.990 line:100%
Nobody knows how to approach it,

22
00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.780 line:100%
because with Chris, it's
always the same.

23
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.360 line:100%
We have to figure out ways to do

24
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.740 line:100%
these kind of things in camera.

25
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.490 line:100%
And every time
when you get sort of

26
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:05.450 line:100%
overwhelmed by it,
you take a step back

27
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:06.750 line:100%
and you make it a 
little bit simpler,

28
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.500 line:100%
you know. And slowly, you know,
a scene like this sort of,

29
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.040 line:100%
grows and comes to life.

30
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.960 line:100%
We try to get permission
to shoot at the actual Trinity

31
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.010 line:100%
test site at White Sands
Proving Grounds.

32
00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:22.470 line:100%
Unfortunately, as a proving
ground is busy with bombing

33
00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.470 line:100%
runs and, you know,
all these different things,

34
00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.680 line:100%
we weren't able to physically
fit in their schedule.

35
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.980 line:100%
I got with the scouts
and I said, okay, well,

36
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.850 line:100%
this whole stretch
looks the same,

37
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.020 line:100%
essentially. It's the same
mountain range.

38
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.240 line:100%
And so we moved 60 miles north.

39
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.740 line:100%
So we were in the exact

40
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.280 line:100%
placement, just literally
60 miles up here.

41
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.870 line:100%
At Trinity there's so much 
you can dig into as far

42
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:48.080 line:100%
as schematics and
the way that the telephone poles

43
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.910 line:100%
were laid out and the wiring.

44
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.670 line:100%
We just followed the research

45
00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.330 line:100%
in terms of dressing
the interior bunker.

46
00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:55.090 line:100%
The really cool thing 
is you could

47
00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.960 line:100%
physically go into the bunker
and go up to the window

48
00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.720 line:100%
and there's your tower and,

49
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.300 line:100%
you know, we could cheat
the distance in the lens.

50
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.340 line:100%
It was pretty unreal.

51
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.970 line:100%
Who builds a 102 foot
replica of the Trinity Tower?

52
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.770 line:100%
There's so much historical
stuff out there.

53
00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.350 line:100%
You want to make sure
it looks right

54
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.650 line:100%
and you just do the research.

55
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.020 line:100%
Building the gadget itself,

56
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.070 line:100%
that was fun.

57
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.480 line:100%
We're able to kind of help

58
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.240 line:100%
tell a little bit of that story
of how it gets assembled,

59
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.410 line:100%
the core and the 
plutonium and all that,

60
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.320 line:100%
you know, and how
it all goes together.

61
00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:27.780 line:100%
Chris likes everything
kind of for real,

62
00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.790 line:100%
you know, like the winch

63
00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.870 line:100%
and everything set up for
the bomb was pretty legit

64
00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:33.210 line:100%
the way it was.

65
00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.500 line:100%
Walking on Ruth’s set,

66
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.170 line:100%
that's where I really felt

67
00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.840 line:100%
this is what they
would have seen.

68
00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.840 line:100%
This is really what
this looked like.

69
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.800 line:100%
This is where they detonated
an atomic bomb.

70
00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.800 line:100%
And we think there's a chance

71
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.970 line:100%
that when we push that button,
we destroy the world.

72
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:49.350 line:100%
Chances are near zero.

73
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.520 line:100%
Near zero.

74
00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:52.520 line:100%
What do you want 
from theory alone?

75
00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.020 line:100%
Zero would be nice.

76
00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:58.110 line:100%
It's an extraordinary moment
in human history.

77
00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:58.860 line:100%
And I wanted

78
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.690 line:100%
to take the audience
into that room

79
00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.740 line:100%
and be there
for that conversation

80
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.240 line:100%
and be there
when that button is pushed.

81
00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.450 line:100%
You're dealing with the biggest

82
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.990 line:100%
themes, the biggest 
of moral dilemmas.

83
00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.250 line:100%
And what's at stake, you know,
what's at stake is insane.

84
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.170 line:100%
One of the trickiest
things was to kind of plot

85
00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.250 line:100%
Oppenheimer's
sort of moral journey

86
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:19.130 line:100%
through all of this,

87
00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.550 line:100%
because he's kind of dancing
between the raindrops

88
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:25.260 line:100%
of this kind of moral puzzle
that he was confronted with.

89
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:26.720 line:100%
It's happening, isn't it?

90
00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.390 line:100%
Cillian’s performance is
so mercurial and so subtle

91
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.180 line:100%
that you're kept
guessing as to what

92
00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:35.560 line:100%
this guy's intentions
really are, because you see him

93
00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.610 line:100%
become the king of the hill
in Los Alamos,

94
00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.440 line:100%
and quite like it.

95
00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.490 line:100%
It was a very conscious thing
for Oppenheimer,

96
00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:46.030 line:100%
he was very consciously
creating his own iconography.

97
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.860 line:100%
It wasn't an accident,

98
00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.780 line:100%
the hat and the pipe.
That was very thought through.

99
00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.370 line:100%
I think the silhouette is one
of the most important signatures

100
00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.000 line:100%
that you could create
for a character.

101
00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.960 line:100%
The silhouette is
what takes the audience

102
00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.220 line:100%
through their journey of the
story that's before them.

103
00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.930 line:100%
There are no real color
photos of Robert Oppenheimer.

104
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.850 line:100%
There is a colorized photo
in Life magazine and from that,

105
00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.480 line:100%
the most important element
was the blue shirt.

106
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.270 line:100%
And so we decided that

107
00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:21.900 line:100%
Robert Oppenheimer
will always wear a blue shirt.

108
00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:25.070 line:100%
Our continuity was really aging.

109
00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.740 line:100%
Some people had four
or five different looks.

110
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.240 line:100%
I think the challenge is
to, on IMAX, create

111
00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.960 line:100%
all of these known people
and do the journey

112
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.460 line:100%
of their life on film,
but with that kind of detail.

113
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.420 line:100%
I think that's
the biggest challenge.

114
00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:46.380 line:100%
I ended up going to an optician
and got a pair of my glasses

115
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.550 line:100%
magnified, and then I do
my checks with those glasses,

116
00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:51.850 line:100%
which have ended up

117
00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:54.010 line:100%
in a funny way,
being called the IMAX glasses.

118
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:55.520 line:100%
Detonator’s charged.

119
00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.100 line:100%
What I knew from experience,
having done, you know,

120
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.980 line:100%
a nuclear explosion via
computer graphics

121
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.690 line:100%
in Dark Knight Rises,
which worked

122
00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.480 line:100%
very well for that film,
and I was very pleased

123
00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.650 line:100%
with everybody's
work on that film.

124
00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.490 line:100%
It showed me that with
a real life event like Trinity,

125
00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:14.740 line:100%
that's as well documented,
the footage is very accessible,

126
00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.500 line:100%
that computer graphics
would never give you

127
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.710 line:100%
the sense of threat that you
see in the real life footage.

128
00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:27.550 line:100%
You know right away you're not

129
00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:28.590 line:100%
doing the explosion
that they did.

130
00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:32.180 line:100%
So you got to figure out a way
that you're going to at least

131
00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:32.840 line:100%
get something big,

132
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.680 line:100%
something we could repeat from
different angles

133
00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:34.850 line:100%
and was going to kind of
get it right.

134
00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:38.640 line:100%
[On set chatter]

135
00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.310 line:100%
[On set chatter]

136
00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.440 line:100%
Because, you know, people
definitely take a better

137
00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.360 line:100%
look at his movies

138
00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.650 line:100%
and especially the accuracy,
on a film like this.

139
00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:50.110 line:100%
So on this show a lot 
the effects work

140
00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:51.700 line:100%
is, you know, special effects

141
00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.910 line:100%
and visual effects
very much bundled together.

142
00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.030 line:100%
Andrew, together 
with Scott Fisher,

143
00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:59.250 line:100%
their whole sort of unit
was one big science project.

144
00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.250 line:100%
This is a thing

145
00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:05.670 line:100%
for simulating the shockwave
from the atomic bomb.

146
00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:08.880 line:100%
There's a slider
to cover this bed

147
00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.430 line:100%
in thin dust
and then release that.

148
00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:15.350 line:100%
And these as little wires slide
under the dust it

149
00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:16.930 line:100%
kicks up like a little shockwave

150
00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.470 line:100%
The two of them

151
00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:22.140 line:100%
did a lot of R&amp;D, they did a lot
of experimentation.

152
00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.610 line:100%
They came up with some
very interesting analog methods.

153
00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:26.940 line:100%
Two, one, zero.

154
00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:28.610 line:100%
A lot of different
types of explosions,

155
00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:30.990 line:100%
a lot of different frame
rates, approaches to how to film

156
00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.070 line:100%
it with the camera, a lot

157
00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.910 line:100%
a lot of different things
going into that sequence.

158
00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:38.910 line:100%
On top we have
about 60 gallons of gasoline

159
00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:40.580 line:100%
with some high explosive.

160
00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:41.830 line:100%
And we detonate that

161
00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.000 line:100%
and then about 2/10
of a second later,

162
00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:47.630 line:100%
we detonate some aluminum
powder for some bright flash.

163
00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:49.920 line:100%
And then about almost a half
a second later,

164
00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.300 line:100%
we detonate the mortar

165
00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.640 line:100%
at the bottom, which is gasoline
and diesel and black powder. And

166
00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.140 line:100%
that pushes up through to kind
of create our mushroom cloud.

167
00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:58.470 line:100%
We're hot. Here we go.

168
00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.980 line:100%
Roll cameras. Rolling,
rolling. Speed!

169
00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.440 line:100%
[On set chatter]

170
00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.690 line:100%
I think for modern day effects,

171
00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:07.980 line:100%
this is kind of bigger
than normal.

172
00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.190 line:100%
Back in the day,
a lot of this was commonplace

173
00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:12.360 line:100%
on a lot of movies,
but I think now they-

174
00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:13.570 line:100%
well, not quite this big.

175
00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.660 line:100%
It's also all about buildup.

176
00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.660 line:100%
Jennifer, she's a brilliant
editor with the help

177
00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.880 line:100%
of like classic film
language and editing.

178
00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.170 line:100%
You know, they build
 up this crescendo

179
00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:31.800 line:100%
that is really, really,

180
00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.760 line:100%
really, really impressive
in so many different ways.

181
00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.680 line:100%
For me, it's like the lead up to
that was the most interesting.

182
00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.800 line:100%
And like, you know these guys,
you've been through it

183
00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.600 line:100%
with them. Learning their faces
and you know who looks like what

184
00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:45.810 line:100%
and who looks scared, and
who looks super confident.

185
00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:49.150 line:100%
So you're like rooting for them
and you want them to be safe.

186
00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.530 line:100%
There's a point where
the music takes a big shift

187
00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:56.490 line:100%
and that's when they're getting
ready to do the final test.

188
00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:00.120 line:100%
And the whole musical tone
just completely changes.

189
00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.910 line:100%
Going from things
that sound like music to things

190
00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:04.950 line:100%
that doesn't sound like music.

191
00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.080 line:100%
So I'm using all these
different sound effects

192
00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.460 line:100%
and making that
into a very stressful emotion,

193
00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:15.090 line:100%
using it as- using music
more like sound design.

194
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.260 line:100%
You can feel it
more in your body

195
00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:22.680 line:100%
than in your ears,
and it just feels very uneasy.

196
00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:25.020 line:100%
It's an interesting
kind of point

197
00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:28.770 line:100%
where you can almost like breath
out, but with a big tension.

198
00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:33.360 line:100%
With the Trinity test, I
approached it moment by moment.

199
00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.070 line:100%
I found first hand

200
00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:38.360 line:100%
descriptions really intriguing
and really inspiring.

201
00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.950 line:100%
It was really just all the 
little details, all the little

202
00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:44.370 line:100%
bits of machinery
they used.

203
00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:46.620 line:100%
Old analog
equipment humming.

204
00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.840 line:100%
All this equipment is more
or less period accurate.

205
00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:55.460 line:100%
You know, the solenoid activates
the detonator,

206
00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:58.090 line:100%
but all these sounds
are just meant to kind of

207
00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.720 line:100%
keep us in the 
suspense of waiting.

208
00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.430 line:100%
There are moments
that demand silence.

209
00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.020 line:100%
It's kind of counterintuitive,
because in movies you expect

210
00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.150 line:100%
to hear the explosion.

211
00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.100 line:100%
when you see the explosion.

212
00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:14.690 line:100%
When it does go kaboom,
you have been lulled

213
00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:18.990 line:100%
into this sense of astonishment
that is all about playing

214
00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:21.990 line:100%
contrast to get the reaction
out of the audience.

215
00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.870 line:100%
Our work here will ensure a
peace mankind has never seen.

216
00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.040 line:100%
Until somebody builds
a bigger bomb.

217
00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:34.170 line:100%
What we were able to do
for the Trinity test

218
00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.670 line:100%
and get into the finished film, 
to me is extraordinary,

219
00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.050 line:100%
to me is extraordinary, 
beautiful

220
00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.050 line:100%
but also very frightening.

